  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\nIssued at 114 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nUpper level ridging continues to build over the region Monday.\nThis pattern shift behind Isaias will lead to warming\ntemperatures and dry conditions through Tuesday and much of\nWednesday. So, expect high temperatures in the low 90s through at\nleast Wednesday with lows dropping into the 60s. Temperatures\nthis high will flirt with record highs, especially Tuesday where\ntwo out of our four climate sites are forecast to either tie or\nbreak all time daily high temperature records. Additionally,\nsoutherly flow will return today, bringing moisture back into the\nregion. Heat indices will increase in tandem, approaching 100 F\nfor many areas Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nWednesday will see the return of more unsettled weather,\nparticularly across portions of northern Arkansas, the Missouri\nBootheel, and northern West Tennessee. An axis of moderate MUCAPE\n(~1000 J/kg) will set up within the zone of higher surface\nmoisture advecting north and east. A surface front will then sag\nsouth, becoming stationary north of the region, but will be\nenough to act as a source of lift to produce isolated to\nscattered convection through Wednesday evening. Forecast\nsoundings across the board point to weak shear around 20 knots.\nSo, severe probabilities are likely minimal with predominantly\nisolated gusty winds from multi-cellular clusters across the\naforementioned areas. Surface moisture is then forecast to lift\nnorth of the region, bringing most of the instability and\nthunderstorms with it, signaling the end of precipitation chances\nfor this week Thursday.\n\nGuidance across the board continues to favor the development of a\nstrong shortwave trough over the northern Plains and Midwest come\nthis Friday. At the surface, a cold front, likely polar in\nnature, will sweep southeastward across the eastern CONUS late\nthis week. Eventually, the front will arrive in the Mid-South,\noffering the first real taste of fall-like weather this year.\nNormally fronts this time of year produce precipitation, but QPF\nremains around zero. So, temperatures will become very mild this\nweekend, with highs dropping into the 70s for many Friday and\ninto the 60s and 70s through the end of the forecast. Lows are\nexpected to be in the 40s and 50s Saturday, dropping into the 40s\nSunday and Monday with areas in northern West Tennessee possibly\ndropping into the 30s.",
